var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-overview","page":"Overview","text":"This package is structured as an umbrella module re-exporting three submodules:\n\nGBGPeriodicFields: the top-level module and public re-export surface.\nFields: grids, Cartesian fiber types, field representations, FFT transforms, field utilities, and broadcasting support.\nFieldAlgebra: fiberwise algebraic operations on scalar, vector, and tensor fields.\nDifferentialOperators: spectral differential operators on fields in spectral representation.\n\nPages = [\n  \"api/GBGPeriodicFields.md\",\n  \"api/Fields.md\",\n  \"api/FieldAlgebra.md\",\n  \"api/DifferentialOperators.md\",\n]\nDepth = 2","title":"API overview"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#DifferentialOperators","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"The DifferentialOperators module provides spectral differential operators on the fields defined in Fields: gradient, divergence, curl, laplacian, inverse_laplacian, and helmholtz_projection, each with an in-place ! form and an allocating form.\n\nThe operators act on fields in spectral representation and are coefficientwise: differentiation is multiplication by the wavenumber multipliers held in a FieldSpectralMultipliers (built from a field with FieldSpectralMultipliers(field), or from the explicit six-array constructor). The laplacian, inverse_laplacian, and helmholtz_projection are representation-preserving; gradient/divergence/curl change tensor order as the operator dictates.\n\nThe Nyquist convention is handled by the multipliers (see FieldSpectralMultipliers in Fields): first-derivative wavenumbers are zeroed at the Nyquist mode on even-length axes, while squared wavenumbers keep the true k².","title":"DifferentialOperators"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#Contents","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"Pages = [\"DifferentialOperators.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#Index","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"Modules = [GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators]","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#Public-API","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"","title":"Public API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#Functions","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#Internal-API","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"The coefficientwise spectral kernels and the aliasing-aware wrappers, documented for contributors.","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#Functions-2","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"module","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"DifferentialOperators\n\nDifferential operators on scalar, vector, and tensor fields on three-dimensional rectangular periodic domains.\n\nThe implemented methods act on fields in spectral representation. Spectral coefficients are used as the implementation representation for Fourier differentiation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.curl","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"curl(field, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocate and return the curl of a vector field in spectral representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.curl"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.curl!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"curl!(\n  destination::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nCompute the curl of a vector field in spectral representation in-place.\n\nThis computes the vector field   u coefficientwise in spectral representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.curl!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.divergence","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"divergence(field, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocate and return the divergence of a field in spectral representation.\n\nFor a vector field in spectral representation, this returns a scalar field in spectral representation. For a second-order tensor field in spectral representation, this returns a vector field in spectral representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.divergence"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.divergence!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"divergence!(\n  destination::ScalarFieldSpectral,\n  field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\ndivergence!(\n  destination::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  field::TensorFieldSpectral{2},\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nCompute the divergence of a field in spectral representation in-place.\n\nFor a vector field u, this computes the scalar field ∇ ⋅ u = ∂ᵢuᵢ.\n\nFor a second-order tensor field A, this computes the vector field with components (∇ ⋅ A)ᵢ = ∂ⱼAᵢⱼ.\n\nFourier differentiation is coefficientwise, using the wavenumber arrays stored in multipliers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.divergence!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.gradient","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"gradient(field, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocate and return the gradient of a field in spectral representation.\n\nFor a scalar field in spectral representation, this returns a vector field in spectral representation. For a vector field in spectral representation, this returns a second-order tensor field in spectral representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.gradient"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.gradient!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"gradient!(\n  destination::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  field::ScalarFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\ngradient!(\n  destination::TensorFieldSpectral{2},\n  field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nCompute the gradient of a field in spectral representation in-place.\n\nFor a scalar field φ, this computes the vector field with components (∇φ)ᵢ = ∂ᵢφ.\n\nFor a vector field u, this computes the second-order tensor field with convention (∇u)ᵢⱼ = ∂ⱼuᵢ.\n\nFourier differentiation is coefficientwise, using the wavenumber arrays stored in multipliers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.gradient!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.helmholtz_projection","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"helmholtz_projection(field::VectorFieldSpectral, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocate and return the Helmholtz projection of a vector field in spectral representation.\n\nThe returned vector field is the divergence-free part of field, with the zero Fourier mode left unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.helmholtz_projection"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.helmholtz_projection!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"helmholtz_projection!(\n  destination::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nCompute the Helmholtz projection of a vector field in spectral representation in-place.\n\nThis operation projects a vector field onto its divergence-free part coefficientwise in spectral representation. For nonzero wavevectors it applies\n\nwidehat(mathbbPu)_i(k) = left(delta_ij - k_i k_j  k^2right) hat u_j(k).\n\nThe zero Fourier mode is left unchanged.\n\nReturns the modified destination field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.helmholtz_projection!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_laplacian","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"inverse_laplacian(field::AbstractFieldSpectral, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocate and return the zero-mean inverse Laplacian of a field in spectral representation.\n\nThis operation is representation-preserving: scalar fields return scalar fields, vector fields return vector fields, and tensor fields return tensor fields of the same tensor order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_laplacian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_laplacian!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"inverse_laplacian!(\n  destination::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n  field::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nCompute the zero-mean inverse Laplacian of a field in spectral representation in-place.\n\nThis operation is representation-preserving and coefficientwise in spectral representation. For every scalar, vector, or tensor component it computes\n\nwidehatDelta^-1 f(k) = - hat f(k)  k^2\n\nfor nonzero wavevectors and sets the zero Fourier mode to zero.\n\nReturns the modified destination field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_laplacian!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.laplacian","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"laplacian(field::AbstractFieldSpectral, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocate and return the Laplacian of a field in spectral representation.\n\nThis operation is representation-preserving: scalar fields return scalar fields, vector fields return vector fields, and tensor fields return tensor fields of the same tensor order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.laplacian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.laplacian!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"laplacian!(\n  destination::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n  field::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nCompute the Laplacian of a field in spectral representation in-place.\n\nThis operation is representation-preserving and coefficientwise in spectral representation. For every scalar, vector, or tensor component it computes\n\nwidehatDelta f(k) = - k^2 hat f(k).\n\nReturns the modified destination field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.laplacian!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_derivative!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Type{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_apply_spectral_derivative!(destination_component, source_component, wavenumber, ::Type{T})\n\nApply a coefficientwise first derivative along one axis: destination_component .= im * wavenumber .* source_component, where wavenumber is a _wavenumber_broadcast_view. The building block for gradient and divergence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_derivative!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_divergence!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Type{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_apply_spectral_divergence!(destination_component, source_1, source_2, source_3, wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3, ::Type{T})\n\nApply a coefficientwise contraction with the wavenumber vector: destination .= im * (k₁ s₁ + k₂ s₂ + k₃ s₃). Used by divergence for the vector → scalar case and per output component for tensor → vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_divergence!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_helmholtz_projection!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Type{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_apply_spectral_helmholtz_projection!(d_1, d_2, d_3, source_1, source_2, source_3, k_1, k_2, k_3, squared_wavenumber, ::Type{T})\n\nApply the coefficientwise Leray/Helmholtz projection onto the divergence-free subspace: subtract the longitudinal part kᵢ (kⱼ ŝⱼ) / |k|² from each component. The zero Fourier mode is left unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_helmholtz_projection!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_inverse_laplacian!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Type{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_apply_spectral_inverse_laplacian!(destination_data, source_data, squared_wavenumber, ::Type{T})\n\nApply the coefficientwise zero-mean inverse Laplacian: -source_data ./ squared_wavenumber for nonzero wavevectors, and zero at the zero Fourier mode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_inverse_laplacian!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_laplacian!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_apply_spectral_laplacian!(destination_data, source_data, squared_wavenumber)\n\nApply the coefficientwise Laplacian: destination_data .= -squared_wavenumber .* source_data, where squared_wavenumber is _squared_wavenumber_broadcast. Representation-preserving over every component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_laplacian!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._curl_noalias!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{VectorFieldSpectral{T, Â, G} where {Â<:AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4}, G<:Grid3D{T}}, VectorFieldSpectral{T, Â, G} where {Â<:AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4}, G<:Grid3D{T}}, FieldSpectralMultipliers}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_curl_noalias!(destination::VectorFieldSpectral, field::VectorFieldSpectral, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nCoefficientwise curl ∇ × u, written componentwise as im * (k × û), assuming destination does not alias field. The public curl! wraps this with the aliasing guard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._curl_noalias!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._helmholtz_projection_noalias!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{VectorFieldSpectral{T, Â, G} where {Â<:AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4}, G<:Grid3D{T}}, VectorFieldSpectral{T, Â, G} where {Â<:AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4}, G<:Grid3D{T}}, FieldSpectralMultipliers}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_helmholtz_projection_noalias!(destination::VectorFieldSpectral, field::VectorFieldSpectral, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nApply the Helmholtz projection (see _apply_spectral_helmholtz_projection!) assuming destination does not alias field. The public helmholtz_projection! wraps this with the aliasing guard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._helmholtz_projection_noalias!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/GBGPeriodicFields/#GBGPeriodicFields.jl","page":"GBGPeriodicFields","text":"","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/GBGPeriodicFields/#Re-exported-modules","page":"GBGPeriodicFields","text":"The top-level module includes and re-exports the public interface of:\n\nGBGPeriodicFields.Fields\nGBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra\nGBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators","title":"Re-exported modules"},{"category":"module","location":"api/GBGPeriodicFields/#GBGPeriodicFields.GBGPeriodicFields","page":"GBGPeriodicFields","text":"GBGPeriodicFields\n\nTools for representing, transforming, and manipulating scalar, vector, and tensor fields on three-dimensional rectangular periodic domains.\n\nThe package is aimed primarily at fluid-dynamics and turbulence simulations, with physical-space and real-to-complex spectral representations designed for both CPU and GPU array backends. It also targets Lagrangian particle simulations through interpolation tools for evaluating fields between grid nodes.\n\nThe package provides field representations, FFT transform plans, storage-wise broadcasting, and algebraic operations on fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.GBGPeriodicFields"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#Testing-—-GBGPeriodicFields","page":"Testing","text":"This is the companion to ARCHITECTURE.md: where that document explains why the package is built the way it is, this one explains how the tests are built and what they must cover. Read it before adding or changing tests. The goal is not a test count — it is that every claim the package makes is checked against something independent of the package.","title":"Testing — GBGPeriodicFields"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#1.-Philosophy","page":"Testing","text":"Verify against independent ground truth, never against the code's own other path. A test that compares one package routine to another package routine only proves they agree, not that either is correct. Two kinds of oracle are used:\n\nAnalytic oracles — for the differential operators. A field is taken to spectral representation, the operator applied, and the result compared to a known closed form: ∂ₓ sin x = cos x, Δ sin x = −sin x, ∇ × (0,0,sin x) = (0,−cos x,0), a Helmholtz-projected field is divergence-free. These catch sign, axis, and scaling errors that a self-consistent round-trip would silently reproduce.\nTrusted reference — for representation parity and for the GPU path. The CPU path is pinned by analytic oracles and the parity baseline; then another computation of the same quantity (the spectral form, or the same operation on a device array) is required to match it. This is still \"two computations of one quantity,\" not a thing against itself.\nStructural identities — relations the operators must satisfy for any input: the vector-calculus identities (div(curl) = 0, curl(grad) = 0) and, on a periodic box, the adjoint relations (divergence as the negative gradient adjoint, self-adjoint curl, a self-adjoint negative-definite Laplacian, a self-adjoint idempotent Helmholtz projection). Tested on randomized band-limited fields, they pin the discrete energy/entropy structure of the scheme.\n\nThe public method set is the spec. Two mechanisms enforce this:\n\nthe parity baseline (test/method_parity.jl) locks the per-generic method counts (198 public methods) so a code-generation change cannot silently add or drop a method;\nintentional-absence tests assert that invalid combinations do not exist (@test_throws MethodError), symmetrically across representations: nonlinear products are absent in spectral (field×field, division by a scalar field, pointwise norms), and the differential operators are absent in physical (differentiation is spectral-only — there are no finite-difference physical operators yet). A deliberate gap is a tested gap.\n\n\"Should work\" is not done. The minimum bar for any change is: the package loads, the parity baseline and ambiguity check pass, and the suite is green.","title":"1. Philosophy"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#2.-Layout","page":"Testing","text":"test/runtests.jl includes each module's file inside its own wrapper module (module FieldAlgebraTests; include(\"fieldalgebra.jl\"); end), so per-file helpers and constants (comp, plans_for, grid, T) cannot collide. Every file is also runnable on its own (julia --project=. test/<file>.jl).\nPer-module behaviour files — fields.jl, fieldalgebra.jl, differentialoperators.jl, plus eltype_genericity.jl (Float64/Float32), adjointness.jl (operator structure on random fields), aqua.jl (package checks), threading.jl (CPU threading; runs in both the default and a JULIA_NUM_THREADS lane), and jet.jl (JET @test_call static analysis of the public operations).\ntest/method_parity.jl — the spec-lock: per-generic method counts plus detect_ambiguities (from Test) over the whole package. The explicit multiply! quartet exists precisely to keep that ambiguity set empty.\ntest/Project.toml — the test environment's dependencies, deliberately backend-free (see §5).\ntest/gpu/ — the opt-in GPU suite (see §5).","title":"2. Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#3.-Writing-a-test-here","page":"Testing","text":"Helpers and conventions. Build fields from coordinate functions (ScalarFieldPhysical((x,y,z) -> …, grid)); take component slices with the local comp/tcomp helpers; build matching transform plans with a plans_for(field) helper that allocates the spectral scratch with similar(field_data(field), …) so it inherits the field's backend. Compare with field_data(result) ≈ expected.\n\nTolerances — the part that bites. ≈ (isapprox) uses a relative tolerance with atol = 0 by default:\n\nFor an O(1) result, bare ≈ is correct.\nFor a result that is mathematically ~zero, bare ≈ degenerates to an almost-exact comparison and will fail on nothing but rounding. Either assert maximum(abs, data) < 1e-12, or pass an explicit atol. Also choose inputs so the expected result is not accidentally zero — e.g. an axis-aligned field (sin x, sin y, sin z) is irrotational and longitudinal, so both curl and the Helmholtz projection return ~0 and test nothing. Use a field whose components depend on more than one coordinate.\nFor cross-backend comparison (GPU vs CPU), the two run the same formula and agree to roundoff, so compare with isapprox(Array(device), host; rtol, atol) carrying a small absolute floor.\nThe same trap appears for inner products of independent fields (e.g. ⟨∇φ, u⟩): they are near-orthogonal, so the common value of two equal inner products is small relative to the operand magnitudes. Scale the floor by the Cauchy–Schwarz product ‖a‖·‖b‖, not by the (tiny) result.\n\nPick the right oracle. Operators → analytic closed form. Representation parity and backend parity → trusted CPU reference. Never assert an operator only by inverting itself.","title":"3. Writing a test here"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#4.-The-coverage-contract","page":"Testing","text":"For each public operation, a test must establish:\n\n[ ] Physical-space correctness against an analytic or hand-computed result.\n[ ] Spectral-space behaviour — correctness where the operation is linear in the     field (valid in both representations); an asserted intentional absence     (@test_throws MethodError) where it is nonlinear (physical-only).\n[ ] Both element types — Float64 and Float32. (Float32 is the realistic GPU     precision; consumer GPUs run Float64 far slower.)\n[ ] An anisotropic domain — at least one test with Lx ≠ Ly ≠ Lz, so the 2π/L     wavenumber scaling is actually exercised and a dropped length factor is visible.\n[ ] The in-place ! form with a caller-supplied destination, including the     aliasing branch for alias-guarded operations (cross, transpose, sym/skew/dev,     the curl/Helmholtz wrappers) — and, for mul!, the LinearAlgebra no-alias contract.\n[ ] Both α/β branches for the five-argument kernels — overwrite (β = 0, which must     ignore the destination's prior contents, NaN included) and accumulate (β ≠ 0).\n[ ] GPU parity — the result matches the CPU path on a device array, under     allowscalar(false).\n\nGrid coverage. Use a mixed-parity grid (even and odd axis lengths, e.g. (8, 7, 6)) so the Nyquist split is exercised; where the Nyquist mode matters, include a field that actually populates it and confirm the derivative of a real field stays real.","title":"4. The coverage contract"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#5.-GPU-testing","page":"Testing","text":"GPU testing is opt-in and dependency-free for the package. The default Pkg.test() stays backend-free and portable; a GPU backend is only ever a test dependency. This is possible because the data layer is generic over AbstractArray, FFT plans are built through AbstractFFTs (dispatched on the array type), and allocations inherit their backend via similar — so a field on a ROCArray/CuArray just works.\n\nPer-backend environments live under test/gpu/ (rocm/ now, cuda/ later). Each is instantiated only on matching hardware (AMD at home, NVIDIA at the office); a no-GPU CI box never installs a vendor stack.\nA single backend-agnostic test/gpu/suite.jl holds the test logic. A thin per-backend entry script (test/gpu/<backend>/runtests.jl) supplies to_device, device_functional, GPU_BACKEND, and calls <Backend>.allowscalar(false) before including the suite. With scalar indexing disabled, any stray scalar index into device memory inside a package method throws — turning invariant 6 into a checked guarantee rather than a convention.\nStrategy: run the public surface on device arrays and compare against the trusted CPU result (matches, with an atol floor per §3). If no device is present the suite warns and skips.\nManifests are gitignored (repo convention — none are tracked); each environment reproduces from its Project.toml plus a [compat] bound, via Pkg.instantiate().\n\nRun it on hardware:\n\njulia --project=test/gpu/rocm -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'   # one-time\njulia --project=test/gpu/rocm test/gpu/rocm/runtests.jl\n\nAdding a backend = mirror rocm/ (see test/gpu/README.md); the suite needs no changes.","title":"5. GPU testing"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#6.-Maintenance","page":"Testing","text":"After any code-generation change, re-verify the method set is unchanged: update the counts in test/method_parity.jl only with a deliberate, reviewed reason, and keep detect_ambiguities empty.\nKeep the test summary in CLAUDE.md (roadmap item 4) roughly current.\nAqua.jl (test/aqua.jl) runs Aqua.test_all on every Pkg.test() — ambiguities, unbound type parameters, undefined exports, project/compat consistency, stale deps, type piracy, persistent tasks. Keep it green; among other things it enforces that every dependency carries a [compat] bound.\nJET.jl (test/jet.jl) runs @test_call on the public operations, filtered to the package's own modules — a per-Julia-version static check for inference-detected errors (MethodError, BoundsError, undefined variables). It is left uncapped in test/Project.toml so each CI Julia version resolves a compatible JET. Note it analyzes the running version, so it does not catch version-divergence (that is the CI matrix's job).\nCI (.github/workflows/CI.yml) runs the full suite on push across Julia 1.11 (the compat floor) and stable, at -t1 and -t4 respectively.","title":"6. Maintenance"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#7.-Current-coverage-and-known-gaps","page":"Testing","text":"Honest status, so the contract above reads as the target rather than a description.\n\nCovered: FFT round-trips; spectral multipliers cross-checked against AbstractFFTs.fftfreq/rfftfreq; the Nyquist split both at the multiplier level and end-to-end (the derivative wavenumber zeroed vs the squared wavenumber kept at the Nyquist mode); M2 + M3 algebra and contractions in both representations; the spectral zero-mode constant invariant; the in-place ! forms with caller-supplied destinations and the alias guards (cross!, transpose!, sym!/skew!/dev!, curl!, helmholtz_projection!); the intentional absences in both representations (nonlinear products absent in spectral, differential operators absent in physical) and the physical/spectral symmetry of every linear field/fiber operation; the differential operators by analytic round-trip, including an anisotropic (2π, 4π, 6π) box that exercises the 2π/L wavenumber scaling; the vector-calculus identities div(curl u) = 0 and curl(grad φ) = 0; element-type genericity on CPU (Float64 and Float32, asserting element-type preservation through operations); the 198-method parity baseline and zero ambiguities; Aqua.test_all (ambiguities, unbound type parameters, undefined exports, Project/compat consistency, stale dependencies, type piracy, persistent tasks); the operator adjoint/structural identities on randomized band-limited fields — divergence as the negative gradient adjoint, self-adjoint curl, a self-adjoint negative-definite Laplacian (with the dissipation identity), and a self-adjoint idempotent Helmholtz projection — with the L² inner product computed inline (global L² products are reserved for separate code by design); and GPU parity on ROCm across Float64 and Float32 (74 checks on an AMD Radeon, allowscalar(false)), including the mul! β = 0 / β ≠ 0 branches; and CPU threading — every threaded assignment is bitwise-identical to serial (field ops, the codegen contractions/products including mul!'s β=0/β≠0 branches, transpose/sym/skew/dev, norms, and the differential operators), exercised in both the default and a JULIA_NUM_THREADS=4 lane (test/threading.jl), with the override / cutoff gate and the GPU materialize! fallback also asserted.\n\nGaps:\n\nDoctests — the plumbing exists (doctest = true, DocTestSetup), but no jldoctest examples have been written into the docstrings.\nBroader randomized/fuzz coverage — the structural identities now run on randomized band-limited fields; extending randomized inputs across the rest of the algebra would broaden coverage further.","title":"7. Current coverage and known gaps"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#GBGPeriodicFields-—-Architecture-and-Rationale","page":"Architecture","text":"This document records the architecture of the package and, more importantly, why it is the way it is. It exists so that future work (human or AI) extends the design instead of accidentally reverting it. CLAUDE.md carries the operational summary; this file carries the reasoning. The AI-tips block in src/GBGPeriodicFields.jl carries the conceptual model and writing conventions.","title":"GBGPeriodicFields — Architecture and Rationale"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#1.-Purpose-and-scope","page":"Architecture","text":"The package is the field layer for pseudospectral simulation of incompressible fluid dynamics and turbulence on three-dimensional rectangular periodic domains. It provides scalar, vector, and second-order tensor fields with two storage representations — physical space and real-to-complex (rfft) spectral space — plus FFT transform plans, spectral multipliers, storage-wise broadcasting, fiberwise algebra (FieldAlgebra), and spectral differential operators (DifferentialOperators). Lagrangian particle interpolation is a planned later layer. The same code must run on CPU Arrays and on GPU arrays (CUDA CuArray, AMDGPU ROCArray); development prioritizes GPU correctness and performance first.","title":"1. Purpose and scope"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#2.-The-central-principle:-representation-validity-lives-in-the-method-set","page":"Architecture","text":"A field is one physical object; physical and spectral storage are two representations of it. An operation is offered in a representation only when it is mathematically the same operation on the underlying physical object there:\n\nLinear in the field — addition/subtraction of fields, scaling by real numbers, contractions with constant fibers, transpose, sym/skew/dev: valid in both representations, because the Fourier transform commutes with them.\nAdding constants — in physical space, every grid value shifts; in spectral space, F(f + c) = F(f) + c·N·δ_{k,0} under the unnormalized-forward convention, so only the zero mode changes, scaled by prod(grid_size). This is why _spectral_constant_scale exists and why constant kernels in spectral representation touch a single coefficient (per component).\nNonlinear in the field — products of two fields, division by a scalar field, pointwise norms, contractions of two fields (dot, cross, tensor mul!, inner products): physical space only. The coefficientwise spectral product is not the Fourier representation of the pointwise product (that would be a convolution), so offering it would silently compute the wrong physical operation.\n\nThe consequence is a hard project invariant: a missing method is a statement, not an omission. The 198-method public surface of FieldAlgebra is the machine-checkable encoding of the validity table above. Any refactor must reproduce the method set exactly; it was verified signature-for-signature against the original — M2 (98 methods: add!/subtract!/multiply!/divide!/+/-/*//) and then M3 (the dot/cross/mul!/transpose/norm/sym/skew/dev contraction and decomposition families, plus their * operators), bringing the total to 198 and parity-checked at runtime in test/method_parity.jl.","title":"2. The central principle: representation validity lives in the method set"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#3.-Types-and-memory-layout","page":"Architecture","text":"Grid3D{T} holds domain geometry; RealFourierLayout records the rfft layout. Field types are parameterized by scalar type T<:AbstractFloat: ScalarFieldPhysical/Spectral, VectorFieldPhysical/Spectral, TensorFieldPhysical{2}/TensorFieldSpectral{2}. Constant fiber values are StaticArrays aliases — FiberVector{T} = SVector{3,T}, FiberTensor2{T} = SMatrix{3,3,T,9} (plus FiberTensor3/4) — so fiber-fiber algebra comes from StaticArrays/LinearAlgebra and the package never defines it. StaticArrays is a direct dependency, not just the type-only StaticArraysCore: the package indexes fibers (a[i], A[i, j]) and uses their algebra inside the generated kernels, so the core-types package alone leaves those operations undefined.\n\nData layout is fiber-last: (Nx, Ny, Nz) for scalars, (Nx, Ny, Nz, 3) for vectors, (Nx, Ny, Nz, 3, 3) for tensors; spectral arrays are Complex{T} with the first axis halved per rfft. Rationale: with column-major storage, each component slice data[:, :, :, i(, j)] is one contiguous block. That gives batched per-component FFTs, coalesced GPU access, pure streaming writes for elementwise kernels, and trivially chunkable index ranges for the deferred CPU threading. Component access goes through views (_vector_component_data, _tensor2_component_data); views are lazy, so splicing them into generated formulas costs nothing.","title":"3. Types and memory layout"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.-Spectral-machinery","page":"Architecture","text":"FieldTransformPlans owns the FFT plans (per backend). Forward transforms are unnormalized; inverse transforms carry the normalization (AbstractFFTs convention) — this single convention choice propagates into the zero-mode constant updates described above.\n\nTransform input-preservation contract. The in-place to_physical!/to_spectral! apply their plans through mul!, the no-copy path, so the source field may be overwritten as scratch. This is intrinsic to real↔complex FFTs: FFTW's complex-to-real (irfft) transform destroys its input by default, and the multidimensional case has no input-preserving algorithm at all. It is also backend-divergent — verified that AMD/rocFFT (via AMDGPU.jl) preserves both directions on tested sizes, while FFTW does not. To keep a uniform, portable contract, the allocating to_physical/to_spectral copy their input before transforming, so non-! calls never mutate their argument (the Julia convention) while ! calls stay allocation-light and document that the source may be consumed.\n\nFieldSpectralMultipliers holds six one-dimensional arrays: derivative wavenumbers wavenumber_1..3 and squared_wavenumber_1..3, plus the grid and layout. It lives in Fields, as a sibling of FieldTransformPlans, because it is representation infrastructure (what the spectral axes mean), not a differential operator; it moved out of DifferentialOperators during the refactor. Two constructors by design:\n\nAn explicit constructor taking all six arrays (lengths validated). This makes the type testable in isolation and lets callers control the backend.\nA convenience constructor from a field: wavenumbers 2π·n/L are built on the host and copied to the field's backend via similar + copyto! (no scalar GPU writes).\n\nThe Nyquist split is deliberate and must not be unified. On even-length axes:\n\nDerivative multipliers are zeroed at the Nyquist frequency. The Nyquist mode of a real signal is a pure cosine; multiplying by ik_N would produce a component the real signal cannot represent, and the symmetric, real-preserving choice for its derivative is zero. This is standard pseudospectral practice and keeps derivatives of real fields exactly real.\nSquared multipliers keep the true k² at Nyquist. The Laplacian of the Nyquist mode is perfectly well defined (-k_N²·mode); zeroing it would silently remove grid-scale dissipation and corrupt inverse_laplacian.\n\nReference tests pin this down: multipliers vs fftfreq/rfftfreq, transform round-trips, and a real-field Nyquist end-to-end test (the derivative zeroed at Nyquist vs the squared wavenumber kept) — see docs/TESTING.md.","title":"4. Spectral machinery"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#5.-Validation-design","page":"Architecture","text":"Fields owns compatibility checking, built from atomic Boolean predicates (_same_scalar_type, _same_tensor_order, _same_grid, _same_layout) composed into _check_same_domain, the representation-dispatched _check_field_domain_compatibility (physical-physical and spectral-spectral methods — the dispatch barrier that prevents cross-representation mixing), the public check_field_compatibility, and _check_field_matches_context for transform plans.\n\nHistory: FieldAlgebra used to carry near-duplicate checkers (_check_scalar_field_compatibility, _check_tensor2_vector_field_compatibility). These were removed in favor of direct calls to Fields._check_field_domain_compatibility — an explicit decision against alias names: one concept, one name, imported explicitly.\n\nCheck placement follows one rule: bodies that go through field broadcasting do not pre-check, because the broadcast machinery already enforces compatibility (shape/grid/style promotion); bodies that bypass broadcasting — copy-then-kernel paths, zero-mode updates — check explicitly. This is why, e.g., physical scalar±Real methods are bare broadcasts while their spectral counterparts call check_field_compatibility before _copy_field_data!.","title":"5. Validation design"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#6.-The-code-generation-architecture-of-FieldAlgebra","page":"Architecture","text":"","title":"6. The code-generation architecture of FieldAlgebra"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Why-it-emerged","page":"Architecture","text":"The original FieldAlgebra was 3033 hand-written lines defining ~198 public methods, with per-component kernels unrolled by hand (three statements per vector operation, nine per tensor). The regularity was overwhelming: one mathematical operation expands into 8–32 near-identical methods (two representations × two argument orders × in-place/allocating × vector/tensor). Hand maintenance meant every new operation or convention change had to be edited consistently in dozens of places — a divergence machine. The refactor regenerates the same method set from small tables, so each formula and each convention is written exactly once, while the public surface (and its deliberate absences) stays bit-identical.","title":"Why it emerged"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Layers","page":"Architecture","text":"M1 — expression builders (Code generation section of FieldAlgebra): _vector_component(symbol, kind, i) / _tensor_component(symbol, kind, i, j) produce component-access expressions, with kind = :field (component view) or :fiber (StaticArrays indexing); _sum_terms and _product combine formulas; _vector_kernel_body / _tensor_kernel_body (with alpha_beta::Bool) and _scalar_kernel_body wrap formulas into kernel bodies ending in return destination. With alpha_beta = true the body branches on iszero(β_value) so the pure-overwrite path stays allocation-free.\nM2 — elementwise families (done): flat @eval tables stamp out constant-fiber kernels (an update lambda (target, value) -> :($target .+= $value) applied per component via statement splats), field-field combination and operators, scalar-constant operators, field±fiber (one row per field type carries the kernels and yields eight methods), real scaling, and scalar-field×fiber (rows carry the destination tensor order for similar_field(…, Val(order))). Type parameters enter tables as expressions — :(TensorFieldPhysical{2, T}) with where {T<:AbstractFloat} on the emitted method. Irregular corners stay explicit, each with a comment saying why: the scalar±Real family (physical broadcasts vs zero-mode updates; reversed subtraction is its own operation), the four ambiguity-resolving multiply! signatures, and the physical-only divisions.\nM3 — contraction kernels (done): one formula table per family — dot, cross, tensor-vector and tensor-tensor mul!, transpose, tensor inner products, pointwise norms, sym/skew/dev — fed through the kernel-body builders. Per-family flags: has_beta (α/β accumulation for the mul! families; five-argument forms convert T(α), T(β), three-argument forms delegate one(T), zero(T)) and alias_guard (cross and the unary index-shuffles — transpose, sym, skew, dev — wrap through _guard_noalias!; mul! is exempt and documents the no-alias contract instead, per LinearAlgebra convention). The sym/skew/dev methods on bare FiberTensor2 remain explicit StaticArrays one-liners.\nM4 — public wrappers/operators (done, folded into M3): the allocating forms and the * operators (tensor·vector, tensor·tensor) are generated alongside their mul!/dot/cross/… kernels, completing the 198-method surface.","title":"Layers"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Documentation-policy","page":"Architecture","text":"Generators emit methods only, never docstrings. Each operation has one docstring attached to the bare binding (function f end for package-owned generics; the bare imported name for Base/LinearAlgebra functions — never function f end there, since a missing import would silently create a new generic). Docstrings show a few canonical signatures plus prose about representation validity, including which combinations are intentionally absent. Exhaustive signature lists were dropped: they duplicated the tables and went stale instantly.","title":"Documentation policy"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Verifying-codegen-changes","page":"Architecture","text":"With Julia available, verification is runtime introspection: load the package, compare per-generic methods(f) counts against the committed baseline (test/method_parity.jl), and run detect_ambiguities (from Test) over the package. The full M2+M3 baseline is 198 public methods (e.g. * 28, mul! 30, dot/dot! 10 each, add! 14). (During the no-Julia refactor phase, parity was checked by simulating table expansion and diffing normalized signatures against the original file; runtime introspection supersedes that.)","title":"Verifying codegen changes"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#7.-Aliasing-policy","page":"Architecture","text":"_aliases_destination detects backing-array sharing between the destination and any field operand (constant fibers never alias). _guard_noalias!(kernel!, destination, output_order, operands) runs the kernel into a fresh similar_field and copies back only when aliasing is detected, so the guard is free in the common case. It exists because index-shuffling operations (cross products, transpose, sym/skew/dev) read several source components while writing each destination component — in-place aliasing would consume already-overwritten data. mul! deliberately skips the guard and documents that the destination must not alias the inputs, matching LinearAlgebra.mul! semantics.","title":"7. Aliasing policy"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#8.-Broadcasting","page":"Architecture","text":"Fields broadcast storage-wise: a field in a broadcast stands for its data array, and the field broadcast style (owned by Fields) materializes into field storage. Style promotion is one-sided by design — define a single binary rule per style pair, never symmetric mixed-representation rules. The broadcast layer doubles as the compatibility enforcement for broadcast-mediated methods (§5).","title":"8. Broadcasting"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#9.-Performance-model-and-CPU-threading","page":"Architecture","text":"Elementwise field operations are memory-bandwidth-bound streaming; FFTs dominate asymptotically. Both are now threaded on CPU (shared memory). On GPU, broadcasting and the device FFT libraries already launch parallel kernels, so the CPU threading is dispatched away (below).\n\nFFTs. FFTW threading is baked in at plan time, so the package's __init__ calls FFTW.set_num_threads(Threads.nthreads()) before any FieldTransformPlans is built (set_fft_threads! retunes it). Oversubscription caveat: FFTW's pthreads multiply with Julia's tasks — code that parallelizes across fields and transforms inside each task should build plans under set_fft_threads!(1). The common case (one field, transforms serial in a time loop) wants nthreads() FFTW threads, the default.\n\nElementwise/fiberwise ops. One internal primitive _assign!(destination, bc) in Fields. The generic method is Broadcast.materialize! — the safe polarity: GPU and unknown array wrappers keep the standard fused broadcast, so invariant 6 holds by dispatch, not discipline. The Union{Array, SubArray{…,<:Array}} specialization threads a single instantiated Broadcasted over contiguous chunks (each chunk a fused copyto!; fusion preserved, one memory pass), bitwise-identical to serial because each output index is written once. A serial cutoff (_SERIAL_CUTOFF, default 2^14) keeps small ops — including the one-element zero-mode views — serial. Policy is hybrid: threaded when nthreads() > 1 and above the cutoff, with a task-local ScopedValue override (with_threading(enabled = …)) for nested-parallel callers.\n\nTwo plug points route work through _assign!: the field broadcast style's copyto! (all whole-field dotted ops and the field-level algebra), and the kernel assignments — the codegen body builders emit _assign!(target, …) (via _to_broadcasted, which rewrites a dotted formula into a lazy broadcasted tree at codegen time), and the hand-written FieldAlgebra / DifferentialOperators kernels use the @_assign! macro in place of .=. No types, signatures, or method tables change — the 198-method surface and the zero-ambiguity property are preserved. Discipline: a raw elementwise write must go through _assign!/@_assign!, never a bare .=, or it silently runs single-threaded.\n\nReductions (global norms, energy integrals) remain a separate, later concern: chunk-partial accumulation reorders floating-point adds, so they need their own design and a tolerance-based test, not the bitwise one used here. Expectations: speedup saturates at socket bandwidth over single-core (typically 2–6×; more on multi-socket nodes, where thread pinning and NUMA first-touch matter — ThreadPinning.jl — a consumer concern, since the package allocates serially via similar).","title":"9. Performance model and CPU threading"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#10.-Decision-log","page":"Architecture","text":"Fields refactor: atomic compatibility predicates; shared _check_same_domain; _check_field_matches_context for transforms.\nFieldSpectralMultipliers moved DifferentialOperators → Fields; explicit six-array constructor (validated lengths) plus convenience constructor; host-built wavenumbers copied to the backend; Nyquist split between derivative and squared multipliers (correctness, see §4).\nFieldAlgebra rewritten around code generation with the invariant that the public method set is reproduced exactly; M2 + M3 delivered and parity-verified at runtime (198 methods, the full surface). Validation rewired to direct _check_field_domain_compatibility calls — alias wrappers explicitly rejected.\nDocstring policy: one docstring per operation on the bare binding, curated signatures + validity prose; generators never emit docstrings.\nOperators defined in function op(...) ... end form (parse-proven house style); short form only for unary +.\nGPU-first; CPU threading designed (_assign!, §9) and deliberately deferred.\nRefactor complete: FieldAlgebra loads and is parity-verified (M2 + M3, 198 methods); DifferentialOperators rewired to import FieldSpectralMultipliers and its helpers from Fields, with the umbrella re-export corrected (the old five-argument constructor removed by design); reference test suite in place (test/, Pkg.test() green) including the method-parity baseline. Remaining: deferred CPU threading (§9) and the planned interpolation layer.\nStaticArrays promoted from StaticArraysCore to a direct dependency — the package indexes fibers and uses their algebra in the generated kernels, so the type-only core package left field ± fiber, scalarfield * fiber, and the M3 fiber contractions undefined at runtime (see §3).\nTransform input-preservation contract: in-place to_physical!/to_spectral! may overwrite their source (FFTW c2r destroys input; backend-divergent), so the allocating to_physical/to_spectral copy the input to keep non-! calls non-mutating (see §4).\nGPU testing is opt-in and dependency-free for the package: the data layer is  generic over AbstractArray, FFT plans are built through AbstractFFTs  (backend-dispatched on the array type) and allocations inherit their backend via  similar, so a GPU backend is only ever a test dependency. It lives in  per-backend environments under test/gpu/ (rocm/ now, cuda/ later), each  instantiated only on matching hardware (AMD at home, NVIDIA at the office); a  shared, backend-agnostic suite.jl runs the public surface on device arrays with  allowscalar(false) — turning invariant 6 into a checked guarantee — and compares  against the trusted CPU result. The default Pkg.test() stays backend-free, so it  is portable across CI and every machine.\nTest-suite buildout (beyond the GPU work in item 10): element-type genericity  (Float32 alongside Float64), anisotropic-domain operators (the 2π/L scaling),  in-place and alias-guard coverage, a Nyquist end-to-end test, and the operator  adjointness / structural identities on randomized band-limited fields  (div = -gradᵀ, self-adjoint curl, negative-definite Laplacian, idempotent Helmholtz  projection — the discrete energy/entropy structure). Intentional-absence tests are  symmetric across representations: nonlinear products absent in spectral; the  differential operators absent in physical (spectral-only — no finite-difference  operators yet). Aqua.test_all was added, which required [compat] bounds for every  dependency. Two deliberate deferrals: a global L²/integration reduction is reserved  for separate code (so adjointness tests use an inline L² inner product), and  doctests wait until the package stabilizes and tutorials are written. Coverage detail  lives in docs/TESTING.md.\nCPU threading implemented (§9), realizing the design formerly deferred in items 6/9. A  Fields._assign! primitive threads elementwise/fiberwise assignments on CPU  (Base.Threads, contiguous chunks, bitwise-identical to serial), routed in via the  field broadcast copyto! and the kernel assignments (codegen _to_broadcasted plus the  @_assign! macro for hand-written kernels); FFTW threads are matched to Julia's at load.  Hybrid policy: on when nthreads() > 1 and above a 2^14 serial cutoff, with a  task-local with_threading ScopedValue override; the GPU path stays on the  materialize! fallback. [compat] julia was raised to 1.11 (for ScopedValue).  Reductions remain deferred (separate FP-ordering design).","title":"10. Decision log"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#FieldAlgebra","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"The FieldAlgebra module provides fiberwise, pointwise algebra on the fields defined in Fields. Each operation comes as an in-place ! form and an allocating form (a Base/LinearAlgebra operator where one exists).\n\nThe operation families are:\n\nVector-space operations — add!/subtract! (and +/-), scaling by a real number, and scalar-field multiplication/division (multiply!/divide!, *//).\nContractions — dot!/dot (vector Euclidean and tensor Frobenius inner products; ⋅ alias), cross!/cross (× alias).\nTensor products — mul! for tensor·vector (left and right action) and tensor·tensor, with the five-argument α/β accumulation form; allocating *.\nPermutations and decompositions — transpose!/transpose, sym/skew/dev (and their ! forms).\nNorms — norm!/norm (pointwise absolute value, Euclidean, and Frobenius).\n\nRepresentation validity is the spec. Operations linear in the field (vector-space operations, contractions with a constant fiber, transpose, sym/skew/dev) are valid in both representations. Operations nonlinear in the field (products of two fields, contractions of two fields, pointwise norms) are physical-space only — the coefficientwise spectral product is not the Fourier transform of the pointwise product. Missing combinations are deliberate.\n\nAliasing. mul! follows the LinearAlgebra convention: the destination must not share storage with an input. The index-shuffling operations (cross product, transpose, sym/skew/dev) are alias-guarded and may be called with the destination aliasing an input.","title":"FieldAlgebra"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Contents","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"Pages = [\"FieldAlgebra.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Index","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"Modules = [GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra]","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Public-API","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"","title":"Public API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Functions","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Internal-API","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"The code-generation machinery, documented for contributors adding operations: the expression and kernel-body builders, the contraction formula builders, the aliasing guard, and the constant / zero-mode helpers. The per-operation methods are stamped out from these by @eval tables and are not themselves documented.","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Functions-2","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"module","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"FieldAlgebra\n\nFiberwise and pointwise algebra for the scalar, vector, and second-order tensor fields defined in Fields.\n\nThis module extends arithmetic (+, -, *, /) and the relevant LinearAlgebra operations (dot, cross, mul!, transpose, norm) to fields, together with in-place add!, subtract!, multiply!, divide!, dot!, cross!, mul!, transpose!, norm!, sym!, skew!, and dev!.\n\nField-field and field-constant operations preserve the representation of their arguments: they act pointwise on fields in physical-space representation and coefficient-wise on fields in spectral representation. Operations that are not linear in a field argument (for example multiplying two fields together, or contracting two fields) are defined only in physical-space representation, except where one factor is a compile-time constant fiber, which keeps the operation coefficient-wise.\n\nMost of the method families below are emitted by small code generators defined in the \"Code generation\" section: the per-component formulas are written once, and the generators stamp out the representation, argument-order, and in-place/allocating variants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.add!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"add!(destination, a, b, fields...)\nadd!(destination, field, constant)\nadd!(destination, constant, field)\n\nAdd fields, or a field and a constant, in place; returns destination.\n\nField-field addition is storage-wise and representation-preserving: pointwise for fields in physical-space representation and coefficient-wise for fields in spectral representation.\n\nThe constant variants pair a scalar field with a Real, a vector field with a FiberVector, and a second-order tensor field with a FiberTensor2, in either argument order. In physical-space representation the constant is added at every grid point; in spectral representation only the zero Fourier coefficient changes, scaled by the inverse-transform normalisation, so that the physical values shift by exactly the constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.add!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.cross!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"cross!(destination::VectorFieldPhysical, u::VectorFieldPhysical, v::VectorFieldPhysical)\ncross!(destination::VectorFieldPhysical, u::VectorFieldPhysical, a::FiberVector)\ncross!(destination::VectorFieldSpectral, u::VectorFieldSpectral, a::FiberVector)\n\nCompute a fiberwise cross product u × v in-place; returns destination.\n\nCross products of two fields are nonlinear and defined only in physical-space representation. In spectral representation only the cross product with a constant fiber vector (either argument order) is provided, which keeps the operation coefficient-wise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.cross!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dev","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"dev(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\ndev(A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\ndev(A::FiberTensor2)\n\nAllocate and compute the deviatoric part of a second-order tensor; fiberwise for fields, and a constant fiber tensor for a constant fiber tensor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dev"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dev!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"dev!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\ndev!(destination::TensorFieldSpectral{2}, A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\n\nCompute the deviatoric part dev(A) = A - tr(A) * I / 3 of a second-order tensor field in-place; returns destination. Linear and representation-preserving, so defined for both representations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dev!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.divide!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"divide!(destination, field, scalar::Real)\ndivide!(destination, field, scalar_field)\ndivide!(destination, numerator, scalar_field)\n\nDivide in place; returns destination.\n\nDivision by a real number is representation-preserving and defined in both representations. Division by a scalar field is pointwise and defined only in physical-space representation; the numerator may be a field of any tensor order (the division acts on all of its components), a real number (yielding a scalar field), or a constant FiberVector or FiberTensor2 (yielding the corresponding vector or tensor field).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.divide!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dot!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"dot!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, u::VectorFieldPhysical, v::VectorFieldPhysical)\ndot!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, u::VectorFieldPhysical, a::FiberVector)\ndot!(destination::ScalarFieldSpectral, u::VectorFieldSpectral, a::FiberVector)\ndot!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, B::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\ndot!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, B::FiberTensor2)\ndot!(destination::ScalarFieldSpectral, A::TensorFieldSpectral{2}, B::FiberTensor2)\n\nCompute a fiberwise inner product in-place; returns destination.\n\nFor vector arguments this is the Euclidean scalar product uᵢ vᵢ; for second-order tensors it is the Frobenius double contraction Aᵢⱼ Bᵢⱼ. Either argument order is accepted.\n\nContraction of two fields is nonlinear and defined only in physical-space representation. In spectral representation only contraction with a constant fiber vector or fiber tensor is provided, which keeps the operation coefficient-wise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dot!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.multiply!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"multiply!(destination, scalar::Real, field)\nmultiply!(destination, field, scalar::Real)\nmultiply!(destination, scalar_field, field)\nmultiply!(destination, scalar_field, constant)\n\nMultiply in place by a real number or by a scalar field; returns destination.\n\nMultiplication by a real number is representation-preserving and defined for fields in physical-space and spectral representation, in either argument order.\n\nMultiplication by a scalar field is pointwise and is therefore defined only in physical-space representation when the other factor is a field; in spectral representation the product of two fields is a convolution, which this module does not provide. When the other factor is a constant FiberVector or FiberTensor2 the operation is linear in the scalar field and stays coefficient-wise, so it is defined in both representations and yields the corresponding vector or tensor field. Either argument order is accepted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.multiply!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.norm!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"norm!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, scalar_field::ScalarFieldPhysical)\nnorm!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, u::VectorFieldPhysical)\nnorm!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\n\nCompute the fiberwise norm of a field in physical-space representation in-place; returns destination.\n\nThis is the pointwise absolute value abs(φ) for scalar fields, the Euclidean norm sqrt(uᵢ uᵢ) for vector fields, and the Frobenius norm sqrt(Aᵢⱼ Aᵢⱼ) (not the operator norm) for second-order tensor fields. The norm is nonlinear and is not defined for spectral representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.norm!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.skew","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"skew(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\nskew(A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\nskew(A::FiberTensor2)\n\nAllocate and compute the skew-symmetric part of a second-order tensor; fiberwise for fields, and a constant fiber tensor for a constant fiber tensor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.skew"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.skew!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"skew!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\nskew!(destination::TensorFieldSpectral{2}, A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\n\nCompute the skew-symmetric part skew(A) = (A - transpose(A)) / 2 of a second-order tensor field in-place; returns destination. Linear and representation-preserving, so defined for both representations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.skew!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.subtract!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"subtract!(destination, a, b, fields...)\nsubtract!(destination, field, constant)\nsubtract!(destination, constant, field)\n\nSubtract fields, or a field and a constant, in place; returns destination.\n\nField-field subtraction computes a - b - fields..., storage-wise and representation-preserving. The constant variants mirror add! — a Real for scalar fields, a FiberVector for vector fields, a FiberTensor2 for second-order tensor fields — acting on every grid point in physical-space representation and on the zero Fourier coefficient only in spectral representation. The reversed argument order computes constant - field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.subtract!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.sym","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"sym(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\nsym(A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\nsym(A::FiberTensor2)\n\nAllocate and compute the symmetric part of a second-order tensor; fiberwise for fields, and a constant fiber tensor for a constant fiber tensor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.sym"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.sym!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"sym!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\nsym!(destination::TensorFieldSpectral{2}, A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\n\nCompute the symmetric part sym(A) = (A + transpose(A)) / 2 of a second-order tensor field in-place; returns destination. Linear and representation-preserving, so defined for both representations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.sym!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.cross","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"cross(u::VectorFieldPhysical, v::VectorFieldPhysical)\n\nAllocating fiberwise cross product, returning a vector field. Two vector fields cross only in physical-space representation; with a constant fiber vector the operation stays coefficient-wise and is available in both representations. See cross! for the in-place form. The infix alias × is also exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.cross"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.dot","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"dot(u::VectorFieldPhysical, v::VectorFieldPhysical)\ndot(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, B::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\n\nAllocating fiberwise inner product, returning a scalar field: the Euclidean vector product uᵢ vᵢ or the tensor Frobenius product Aᵢⱼ Bᵢⱼ. Two fields contract only in physical-space representation; with a constant fiber the contraction stays coefficient-wise and is available in both representations. See dot! for the in-place form. The infix alias ⋅ is also exported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.dot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.mul!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"mul!(destination::VectorFieldPhysical, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, u::VectorFieldPhysical[, α, β])\nmul!(destination::VectorFieldPhysical, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, a::FiberVector[, α, β])\nmul!(destination::VectorFieldSpectral, A::FiberTensor2, u::VectorFieldSpectral[, α, β])\nmul!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, B::TensorFieldPhysical{2}[, α, β])\nmul!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, B::FiberTensor2[, α, β])\n\nCompute a fiberwise tensor product in-place; returns destination.\n\nFor a vector destination this is the action of a second-order tensor on a vector: the left action Aᵢⱼ uⱼ, the right action uⱼ Aⱼᵢ. For a tensor destination it is the tensor product (A B)ᵢⱼ = Aᵢₖ Bₖⱼ.\n\nProducts of two fields are nonlinear and defined only in physical-space representation; spectral-space methods require one factor to be a constant fiber tensor or fiber vector. The five-argument form computes destination = α * product + β * destination. As with LinearAlgebra.mul!, destination must not share storage with any input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.mul!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.norm","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"norm(scalar_field::ScalarFieldPhysical)\nnorm(u::VectorFieldPhysical)\nnorm(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\n\nAllocating fiberwise norm, returning a scalar field: the pointwise absolute value for scalar fields, the Euclidean norm for vector fields, and the Frobenius norm for second-order tensor fields. Physical-space representation only. See norm! for the in-place form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.norm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.transpose!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"transpose!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\ntranspose!(destination::TensorFieldSpectral{2}, A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\n\nTranspose the fiber indices of a second-order tensor field in-place: destinationᵢⱼ = Aⱼᵢ; returns destination.\n\nThe operation is linear and representation-preserving, so it is defined for both physical-space and spectral representations. Unlike Julia's matrix transpose, this writes a canonical tensor field rather than a lazy wrapper.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.transpose!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._aliases_destination-Tuple{Any, Vararg{Any}}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_aliases_destination(destination, operands...)\n\nReturn true if destination shares its backing array with any field among operands. Constant fibers are not fields and never alias. Used by _guard_noalias!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._aliases_destination"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._copy_field_data!-Tuple{Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}, Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_copy_field_data!(destination::AbstractField, field::AbstractField)\n\nCopy field's storage into destination's storage unless they already alias; returns destination. Used by copy-then-kernel paths that must not assume the destination begins empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._copy_field_data!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._cross_formula-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_cross_formula(left_symbol, left_kind, right_symbol, right_kind)\n\nReturn a per-component function i -> expr for the cross product: with cyclic (j, k), component i is uⱼ v_k  u_k vⱼ. Feeds _vector_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._cross_formula"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._dot_tensor_rhs-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_dot_tensor_rhs(A_symbol, A_kind, B_symbol, B_kind)\n\nBuild the right-hand-side expression for the tensor Frobenius inner product, Σᵢⱼ Aᵢⱼ Bᵢⱼ, fed to _scalar_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._dot_tensor_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._dot_vector_rhs-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_dot_vector_rhs(u_symbol, u_kind, v_symbol, v_kind)\n\nBuild the right-hand-side expression for a vector inner product, Σᵢ uᵢ vᵢ — a single broadcasted expression fed to _scalar_kernel_body. Operand kinds are :field or :fiber, as in _vector_component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._dot_vector_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._guard_noalias!-Tuple{Any, Any, Val, Tuple}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_guard_noalias!(kernel!, destination, output_order::Val, operands::Tuple)\n\nRun kernel!(destination, operands...) while protecting against destination/input aliasing. If destination aliases any field in operands, run the kernel into a fresh field of tensor order output_order and copy the result back; otherwise run in place. Used by index-shuffling operations (cross product, transpose, sym/skew/dev) that read several source components while writing each destination component. mul! deliberately skips this guard and documents the no-alias requirement instead, matching the LinearAlgebra convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._guard_noalias!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._product-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_product(x, y)\n\nBuild the broadcasted product expression x .* y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._product"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._scalar_field_broadcast_data-Tuple{ScalarFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldPhysical}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_scalar_field_broadcast_data(scalar_field::ScalarFieldPhysical, field::AbstractFieldPhysical)\n\nReshape a scalar field's data with singleton fiber dimensions so it broadcasts against the fiber components of field. Used by pointwise scalar-field multiplication and division so the scalar acts on every component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._scalar_field_broadcast_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._scalar_kernel_body-Tuple{Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_scalar_kernel_body(rhs)\n\nBuild the body of a scalar-field-valued kernel (a contraction with a scalar result): field_data(destination) .= rhs, ending in return destination.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._scalar_kernel_body"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._spectral_constant_scale-Union{Tuple{AbstractFieldSpectral{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_spectral_constant_scale(field::AbstractFieldSpectral)\n\nReturn prod(grid_size), the factor by which a physical constant scales the zero Fourier coefficient under the unnormalized-forward FFT convention. Adding a real constant in spectral representation multiplies it by this scale and writes only the zero mode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._spectral_constant_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._spectral_zero_mode_component_data-Tuple{VectorFieldSpectral, Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_spectral_zero_mode_component_data(field::VectorFieldSpectral, component)\n_spectral_zero_mode_component_data(field::TensorFieldSpectral{2}, first_index, second_index)\n\nReturn a 1×1×1 view of the zero Fourier coefficient of one component of a vector or second-order tensor field in spectral representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._spectral_zero_mode_component_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._spectral_zero_mode_data-Tuple{ScalarFieldSpectral}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_spectral_zero_mode_data(field::ScalarFieldSpectral)\n\nReturn a 1×1×1 view of the zero Fourier coefficient of a scalar field in spectral representation; constant additions in spectral representation write only here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._spectral_zero_mode_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._sum_terms-Tuple{Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_sum_terms(terms)\n\nCombine a non-empty collection of expressions into a broadcasted sum term_1 .+ term_2 .+ ….\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._sum_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._tensor_component-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_tensor_component(symbol, kind, i, j)\n\nBuild the expression accessing component (i, j) of a second-order tensor argument named symbol. kind = :field reads a field's component view (_tensor2_component_data); kind = :fiber indexes a constant SMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._tensor_component"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._tensor_kernel_body-Tuple{Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_tensor_kernel_body(formula; alpha_beta::Bool)\n\nAs _vector_kernel_body, but for a second-order tensor-valued kernel: destination_ij .= formula(i, j) for i, j in 1:3, with the same alpha_beta five-argument behaviour.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._tensor_kernel_body"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._tensor_tensor_formula-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_tensor_tensor_formula(A_symbol, A_kind, B_symbol, B_kind)\n\nReturn a per-component function (i, j) -> expr for the tensor-tensor product, component (i, j) being Σₖ Aᵢₖ Bₖⱼ. Feeds _tensor_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._tensor_tensor_formula"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._tensor_vector_formula-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_tensor_vector_formula(A_symbol, A_kind, u_symbol, u_kind)\n\nReturn a per-component function i -> expr for the tensor-on-vector left action, component i being Σⱼ Aᵢⱼ uⱼ. Feeds _vector_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._tensor_vector_formula"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._transpose_formula-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_transpose_formula(A_symbol, A_kind)\n\nReturn a per-component function (i, j) -> expr for the transpose, component (i, j) being Aⱼᵢ. Feeds _tensor_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._transpose_formula"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._vector_component-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_vector_component(symbol, kind, i)\n\nBuild the expression accessing component i of a vector argument named symbol. kind = :field reads a field's component view (_vector_component_data); kind = :fiber indexes a constant SVector. Spliced into kernel formulas so each per-component formula is written once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._vector_component"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._vector_kernel_body-Tuple{Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_vector_kernel_body(formula; alpha_beta::Bool)\n\nBuild the body of a vector-valued kernel: destination_i .= formula(i) for i in 1:3, where formula(i) is the per-component expression. With alpha_beta = true, emit the five-argument destination = α * formula + β * destination form, branching on iszero(β_value) so the pure-overwrite path stays allocation-free. The body ends in return destination and is spliced into a function binding destination (and, when alpha_beta, α_value/β_value).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._vector_kernel_body"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._vector_tensor_formula-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_vector_tensor_formula(u_symbol, u_kind, A_symbol, A_kind)\n\nReturn a per-component function i -> expr for the vector-on-tensor right action, component i being Σⱼ uⱼ Aⱼᵢ. Feeds _vector_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._vector_tensor_formula"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Fields","page":"Fields","text":"The Fields module defines the field objects and their storage. A field is a physical object; the *Physical and *Spectral types are two representations of the same field. It provides:\n\nGrids and layout — Grid3D for the periodic domain geometry and RealFourierLayout for the real-to-complex transform layout.\nFiber types — the constant Cartesian fiber values FiberVector, FiberTensor2, FiberTensor3, FiberTensor4 (StaticArrays aliases).\nField types — scalar, vector, and second-order tensor fields, each in physical-space and spectral representation.\nTransforms — FieldTransformPlans and to_physical(!) / to_spectral(!). The allocating forms preserve their input; the in-place ! forms may overwrite the source (a property of the real FFT — see their docstrings).\nSpectral multipliers — FieldSpectralMultipliers, the wavenumber arrays used by the spectral differential operators.\nBroadcasting — fields broadcast storage-wise and representation-preserving: a dotted assignment writes into the destination field's storage, while an out-of-place dotted expression allocates a compatible field in the same representation. Physical and spectral representations cannot be mixed in one broadcast.","title":"Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Contents","page":"Fields","text":"Pages = [\"Fields.md\"]\nDepth = 2:3","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Index","page":"Fields","text":"Modules = [GBGPeriodicFields.Fields]","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Public-API","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Public API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Types","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Functions","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Internal-API","page":"Fields","text":"These symbols are not part of the public interface; they are documented for contributors extending the package — field-allocation and compatibility helpers, the spectral-multiplier broadcast helpers, and the broadcast machinery.","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Types-2","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Functions-2","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields","page":"Fields","text":"Fields\n\nField representation and transform core for scalar, vector, and tensor fields on three-dimensional rectangular periodic domains.\n\nThis module defines grids, Cartesian fiber types, Fourier-layout conventions, field types for physical-space representation and real-to-complex spectral representation, FFT transform plans, and storage-wise broadcasting designed for CPU and GPU array backends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractFieldPhysical","page":"Fields","text":"AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R}\n\nAbstract supertype for fields represented by values in physical space.\n\nHere T<:AbstractFloat is the real scalar type associated with the field and R is the tensor order stored as an integer value parameter. Concrete subtypes provide the actual storage and metadata. The generic field interface obtains T and R through field_scalar_type and field_tensor_order, and obtains the stored data through field_data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractFieldPhysical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractFieldSpectral","page":"Fields","text":"AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R}\n\nAbstract supertype for fields represented by Fourier coefficients in spectral space.\n\nHere T<:AbstractFloat is the real scalar type associated with the field and R is the tensor order stored as an integer value parameter. Concrete subtypes provide the actual storage and metadata. The generic field interface obtains T and R through field_scalar_type and field_tensor_order, and obtains the stored data through field_data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractFieldSpectral"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FiberTensor2","page":"Fields","text":"FiberTensor2{T}\n\nStatic second-order Cartesian tensor type used for individual tensor values.\n\nThis is an alias for SMatrix{3, 3, T, 9}, with scalar type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FiberTensor2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FiberTensor3","page":"Fields","text":"FiberTensor3{T}\n\nStatic third-order Cartesian tensor type used for individual tensor values.\n\nThis is an alias for SArray{Tuple{3, 3, 3}, T, 3, 27}, with scalar type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FiberTensor3"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FiberTensor4","page":"Fields","text":"FiberTensor4{T}\n\nStatic fourth-order Cartesian tensor type used for individual tensor values.\n\nThis is an alias for SArray{Tuple{3, 3, 3, 3}, T, 4, 81}, with scalar type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FiberTensor4"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FiberVector","page":"Fields","text":"FiberVector{T}\n\nStatic three-component Cartesian vector type used for individual vector values.\n\nThis is an alias for SVector{3, T}, with scalar type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FiberVector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldSpectralMultipliers","page":"Fields","text":"FieldSpectralMultipliers(\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n  wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3,\n  squared_wavenumber_1, squared_wavenumber_2, squared_wavenumber_3,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nFieldSpectralMultipliers(\n  field::AbstractFieldSpectral{T},\n  wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3,\n  squared_wavenumber_1, squared_wavenumber_2, squared_wavenumber_3,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nOne-dimensional Fourier wavenumber arrays for coefficientwise spectral operators such as derivatives, the Laplacian, and the Helmholtz projection.\n\nThe fields are\n\ngrid, fourier_layout: the Grid3D and RealFourierLayout the multipliers were built for.\nwavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3: per-axis wavenumbers, used for first derivatives via multiplication by im * k.\nsquared_wavenumber_1, squared_wavenumber_2, squared_wavenumber_3: per-axis squared wavenumbers, used for the Laplacian via multiplication by -k^2.\n\nEach array runs along one spatial axis in the FFT frequency ordering of fourier_layout; the half axis fourier_layout.fft_dims[1] stores only the nonnegative frequencies. The arrays must live on storage (CPU or GPU) matching the fields the multipliers are applied to.\n\nThese two constructors are explicit: you supply both the wavenumbers and the squared wavenumbers, and only their lengths are checked. The two array families are stored independently rather than squaring one to obtain the other, because on even grids they use different conventions for the Nyquist mode (the highest representable frequency). A convenience constructor FieldSpectralMultipliers(field) builds both families from the field's grid and layout with the correct Nyquist treatment; it is documented separately below.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldSpectralMultipliers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldSpectralMultipliers-Union{Tuple{AbstractFieldSpectral{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Fields","text":"FieldSpectralMultipliers(field::AbstractFieldSpectral{T}) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nBuild the spectral multipliers from the grid and Fourier layout of field, allocated on the field's storage backend.\n\nThe wavenumbers are the physical angular wavenumbers k = 2π n / L along each axis, where L is the domain length and n runs over the FFT frequency indices. On an even-length axis the highest stored mode is the Nyquist mode: its first-derivative wavenumber is set to zero, so that derivatives of real fields stay real, while its squared wavenumber keeps the true k^2, so that the Laplacian and Poisson-type solves stay correct. On an odd-length axis there is no exact Nyquist mode and no special treatment is applied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldSpectralMultipliers"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldTransformPlans","page":"Fields","text":"FieldTransformPlans(\n  physical::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n  spectral::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R};\n  fft_flags = nothing,\n  fft_timelimit = Inf,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nForward and inverse FFT plans connecting compatible fields represented in physical and spectral space.\n\nHere physical provides the real-space storage array, spectral provides the real-to-complex Fourier coefficient array, fft_flags is an optional backend-specific FFT planner flag, and fft_timelimit is the planner time limit used when fft_flags is provided. Only the spatial dimensions field_layout(physical).fft_dims are transformed; tensor-fiber dimensions are left untransformed.\n\nThe fields are\n\ngrid::G: the Grid3D for which the plans were constructed.\nfourier_layout::L: the RealFourierLayout for which the plans were constructed.\nplan_forward_fft::PF: the plan transforming physical values to spectral coefficients.\nplan_inverse_fft::PI: the plan transforming spectral coefficients to physical values.\n\nThe plans are backend-, array-type-, scalar-type-, and tensor-order-specific; construct a separate FieldTransformPlans object for each CPU/GPU backend and field tensor order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldTransformPlans"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.Grid3D","page":"Fields","text":"Grid3D(\n  grid_size::NTuple{3, Int},\n  domain_size::NTuple{3, T},\n  grid_spacing::NTuple{3, T},\n  origin::NTuple{3, T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nGrid3D(\n  grid_size::NTuple{3, Int},\n  domain_size::NTuple{3, T};\n  origin::NTuple{3, T} = (zero(T), zero(T), zero(T)),\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nUniform three-dimensional grid for a periodic rectangular domain.\n\nThe fields are\n\ngrid_size::NTuple{3, Int}: the number of grid points in each spatial direction.\ndomain_size::NTuple{3, T}: the length of the domain in each spatial direction.\ngrid_spacing::NTuple{3, T}: the spacing between adjacent grid points in each spatial direction.\norigin::NTuple{3, T}: the physical coordinates of the first grid point.\n\nThe two-argument constructor computes grid_spacing = domain_size ./ grid_size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.Grid3D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.RealFourierLayout","page":"Fields","text":"RealFourierLayout()\nRealFourierLayout(fft_dims::NTuple{3, Int})\n\nLayout convention for three-dimensional real-to-complex Fourier transforms.\n\nHere fft_dims is the tuple of transformed dimensions passed to the FFT planner. The main role of the ordering is to specify the real-FFT half dimension: in dimension fft_dims[1], only the nonnegative frequencies are represented, while the other dimensions use the full complex FFT frequency ordering.\n\nThe zero-argument constructor uses fft_dims = (1, 2, 3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.RealFourierLayout"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.ScalarFieldPhysical","page":"Fields","text":"ScalarFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, 3},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nScalarFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, 3},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nScalarFieldPhysical(\n  f,\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout();\n  array_type = Array,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nScalar field in physical-space representation, stored as its values on a three-dimensional physical grid.\n\nHere values_at_grid contains the real scalar values, grid is the Grid3D on which the values are sampled, and fourier_layout is the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field.\n\nThe function constructor constructs a scalar field in physical-space representation by sampling a real-valued function on a three-dimensional physical grid. Here f is called as f(x, y, z), grid is the Grid3D on which the values are sampled, fourier_layout is the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field, and array_type is the array type used to store the sampled values.\n\nBroadcasting over scalar fields in physical-space representation is lifted to values_at_grid. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.values_at_grid, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new ScalarFieldPhysical.\n\nThe two-argument constructors use the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.ScalarFieldPhysical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.ScalarFieldSpectral","page":"Fields","text":"ScalarFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 3},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nScalarFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 3},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nScalar field in spectral representation, stored as its nonredundant real-to-complex Fourier coefficients.\n\nHere fourier_coefficients contains the complex Fourier coefficients in real-FFT storage, grid is the Grid3D associated with the physical domain, and fourier_layout is the RealFourierLayout specifying the transformed dimensions and the real-FFT half dimension.\n\nThe coefficient array has shape spectral_field_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(0)). The dimension fourier_layout.fft_dims[1] is the real-FFT half dimension.\n\nBroadcasting over scalar fields in spectral representation is lifted to fourier_coefficients. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.fourier_coefficients, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new ScalarFieldSpectral. All such operations are coefficientwise; for example, .* does not represent a pseudospectral product of scalar fields.\n\nThe two-argument constructor uses the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.ScalarFieldSpectral"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.TensorFieldPhysical","page":"Fields","text":"TensorFieldPhysical{R}(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {R, T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTensorFieldPhysical{R}(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {R, T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTensorFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, N},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, N}\n\nTensorFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, N},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, N}\n\nTensorFieldPhysical{R}(\n  f,\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout();\n  array_type = Array,\n) where {R, T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTensor field of order R ≥ 2 in physical-space representation, stored as its components on a three-dimensional physical grid.\n\nHere values_at_grid contains the real tensor components with storage order values_at_grid[i, j, k, α₁, ..., αᵣ], where each tensor index has length 3, grid is the Grid3D on which the values are sampled, and fourier_layout is the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field.\n\nThe array has shape physical_field_shape(grid, Val(R)). The constructors without explicit {R} infer R = N - 3 from the array dimension N.\n\nThe function constructor constructs a tensor field of order R ≥ 2 in physical-space representation by sampling a real tensor-valued function on a three-dimensional physical grid. Here f is called as f(x, y, z) and must return an indexable object with tensor-fiber shape fiber_shape(Val(R)), grid is the Grid3D on which the values are sampled, fourier_layout is the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field, and array_type is the array type used to store the sampled values.\n\nBroadcasting over tensor fields in physical-space representation is lifted to values_at_grid. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.values_at_grid, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new TensorFieldPhysical. These operations are storage-wise and componentwise. They do not represent tensor contractions.\n\nThe two-argument constructors use the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.TensorFieldPhysical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.TensorFieldSpectral","page":"Fields","text":"TensorFieldSpectral{R}(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {R, T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTensorFieldSpectral{R}(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {R, T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTensorFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, N},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, N}\n\nTensorFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, N},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, N}\n\nTensor field of order R ≥ 2 in spectral representation, stored as its nonredundant real-to-complex Fourier coefficients.\n\nHere fourier_coefficients contains the complex tensor Fourier coefficients in real-FFT storage, with the tensor indices last: fourier_coefficients[i, j, k, α₁, ..., αᵣ], where each tensor index has length 3. The spatial part has shape spectral_field_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(0)), and the full array has shape spectral_field_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(R)). The dimension fourier_layout.fft_dims[1] is the real-FFT half dimension.\n\nBroadcasting over tensor fields in spectral representation is lifted to fourier_coefficients. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.fourier_coefficients, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new TensorFieldSpectral. These operations are storage-wise and componentwise. They do not represent pseudospectral products or tensor contractions.\n\nThe two-argument constructors use the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.TensorFieldSpectral"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.VectorFieldPhysical","page":"Fields","text":"VectorFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, 4},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nVectorFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, 4},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nVectorFieldPhysical(\n  f,\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout();\n  array_type = Array,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nVector field in physical-space representation, stored as its components on a three-dimensional physical grid.\n\nHere values_at_grid contains the real vector components with storage order values_at_grid[i, j, k, α], where α = 1, 2, 3, grid is the Grid3D on which the values are sampled, and fourier_layout is the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field.\n\nThe function constructor constructs a vector field in physical-space representation by sampling a real vector-valued function on a three-dimensional physical grid. Here f is called as f(x, y, z) and must return a three-component indexable object, grid is the Grid3D on which the values are sampled, fourier_layout is the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field, and array_type is the array type used to store the sampled values.\n\nBroadcasting over vector fields in physical-space representation is lifted to values_at_grid. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.values_at_grid, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new VectorFieldPhysical. These operations are storage-wise and componentwise.\n\nThe two-argument constructors use the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.VectorFieldPhysical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.VectorFieldSpectral","page":"Fields","text":"VectorFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nVectorFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nVector field in spectral representation, stored as its nonredundant real-to-complex Fourier coefficients.\n\nHere fourier_coefficients contains the complex vector Fourier coefficients in real-FFT storage, with the component index last: fourier_coefficients[i, j, k, α], where α = 1, 2, 3. The spatial part has shape spectral_field_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(0)), and the full array has shape spectral_field_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(1)). The dimension fourier_layout.fft_dims[1] is the real-FFT half dimension.\n\nBroadcasting over vector fields in spectral representation is lifted to fourier_coefficients. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.fourier_coefficients, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new VectorFieldSpectral. These operations are storage-wise and componentwise. They do not represent pseudospectral products or tensor contractions.\n\nThe two-argument constructor uses the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.VectorFieldSpectral"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.fiber_shape-Tuple{Val{0}}","page":"Fields","text":"fiber_shape(::Val{R}) where {R}\n\nReturn the tensor-fiber shape associated with tensor order R.\n\nFor scalar fields, R = 0 gives (). For higher tensor orders, each tensor index has length 3, so R = 1 gives (3,), R = 2 gives (3, 3), and so on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.fiber_shape"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_data","page":"Fields","text":"field_data(field::AbstractField)\n\nReturn the array storing the field data.\n\nFor fields represented in physical space, this is the array of values on the grid. For fields represented in spectral space, this is the array of Fourier coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_grid-Tuple{Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}}","page":"Fields","text":"field_grid(field::AbstractField)\n\nReturn the Grid3D associated with a field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_grid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_layout-Tuple{Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}}","page":"Fields","text":"field_layout(field::AbstractField)\n\nReturn the RealFourierLayout associated with a field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_layout"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_scalar_type-Union{Tuple{AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R}}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}} where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}","page":"Fields","text":"field_scalar_type(\n  field::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nfield_scalar_type(\n  field::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nReturn the real scalar type T associated with a field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_scalar_type"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_tensor_order-Union{Tuple{AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R}}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}} where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}","page":"Fields","text":"field_tensor_order(\n  field::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nfield_tensor_order(\n  field::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nReturn the tensor order R of a field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_tensor_order"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.physical_field_shape-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{Grid3D, Val{R}}} where R","page":"Fields","text":"physical_field_shape(grid::Grid3D, ::Val{R}) where {R}\n\nReturn the storage shape of a field of tensor order R represented in physical space on grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.physical_field_shape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.set_fft_threads!-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Fields","text":"set_fft_threads!(n)\n\nSet the number of threads FFTW uses, effective for plans built afterwards (FFTW fixes the count at plan construction). Returns n. Code that parallelizes across many fields at once should build plans under set_fft_threads!(1) to avoid FFTW×Julia thread oversubscription.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.set_fft_threads!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.set_threading_cutoff!-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Fields","text":"set_threading_cutoff!(n)\n\nSet the element-count threshold below which assignments run serially. Returns n. See threading_cutoff.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.set_threading_cutoff!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.spectral_field_shape-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{Grid3D, RealFourierLayout, Val{R}}} where R","page":"Fields","text":"spectral_field_shape(\n  grid::Grid3D,\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n  ::Val{R},\n) where {R}\n\nReturn the storage shape of a field of tensor order R represented in nonredundant real-to-complex spectral space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.spectral_field_shape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.threading_cutoff-Tuple{}","page":"Fields","text":"threading_cutoff()\n\nThe current element-count threshold below which assignments run serially.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.threading_cutoff"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_physical!-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R}, AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R}, FieldTransformPlans{T, R, PF, PI, G} where {PF, PI, G<:Grid3D{T}}}} where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}","page":"Fields","text":"to_physical!(\n  physical::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n  spectral::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n  transform_plans::FieldTransformPlans{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nTransform a field from spectral space to physical space in-place.\n\nHere physical is the destination, spectral is the source, and transform_plans provides the inverse FFT plan.\n\nThe inverse transform uses spectral as scratch space and may overwrite it: on return its coefficients are left in an undefined state (the FFTW CPU backend destroys them — complex-to-real transforms have no input-preserving algorithm in the multidimensional case; some GPU backends happen to preserve them, but this is not guaranteed). Use the allocating to_physical when spectral must be preserved.\n\nReturns the modified destination field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_physical!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_physical-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R}, FieldTransformPlans{T, R, PF, PI, G} where {PF, PI, G<:Grid3D{T}}}} where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}","page":"Fields","text":"to_physical(\n  spectral::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n  transform_plans::FieldTransformPlans{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nAllocating version of to_physical!. Unlike the in-place form, the input spectral is left unchanged: its coefficients are copied before the transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_physical"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_spectral!-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R}, AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R}, FieldTransformPlans{T, R, PF, PI, G} where {PF, PI, G<:Grid3D{T}}}} where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}","page":"Fields","text":"to_spectral!(\n  spectral::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n  physical::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n  transform_plans::FieldTransformPlans{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nTransform a field from physical space to spectral space in-place.\n\nHere spectral is the destination, physical is the source, and transform_plans provides the forward FFT plan.\n\nThe forward transform may overwrite physical on some backends (for example rocFFT reserves the right to use real-to-complex inputs as scratch); the FFTW CPU backend preserves it, but do not rely on the source surviving. Use the allocating to_spectral when physical must be preserved.\n\nReturns the modified destination field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_spectral!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_spectral-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R}, FieldTransformPlans{T, R, PF, PI, G} where {PF, PI, G<:Grid3D{T}}}} where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}","page":"Fields","text":"to_spectral(\n  physical::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n  transform_plans::FieldTransformPlans{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nAllocating version of to_spectral!. Unlike the in-place form, the input physical is left unchanged: its values are copied before the transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_spectral"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.with_threading-Tuple{Any}","page":"Fields","text":"with_threading(f; enabled = true)\n\nRun f() with the CPU-threading decision forced to enabled (true/false, or nothing for the automatic nthreads()-following policy). The override is dynamically scoped and task-local: it affects only f() and is restored afterwards, including on exceptions. Use enabled = false around a region that already parallelizes at a higher level, to avoid oversubscription.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.with_threading"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldPhysicalBroadcastStyle","page":"Fields","text":"FieldPhysicalBroadcastStyle{N}\n\nBroadcast style for fields in physical-space representation (N = 3 + tensor_order). A field stands for its storage array inside a broadcast, and the style materializes the result back into a physical-space field. See also FieldSpectralBroadcastStyle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldPhysicalBroadcastStyle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldSpectralBroadcastStyle","page":"Fields","text":"FieldSpectralBroadcastStyle{N}\n\nBroadcast style for fields in spectral representation (N = 3 + tensor_order); the spectral counterpart of FieldPhysicalBroadcastStyle. Combining the two styles is an error, so physical and spectral representations never mix in one broadcast.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldSpectralBroadcastStyle"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._SERIAL_CUTOFF","page":"Fields","text":"_SERIAL_CUTOFF\n\nElement count below which an assignment runs serially regardless of thread count: threading overhead is not worth it for small arrays, and this exempts the one-element zero-mode views. Read in the assignment hot path; set with set_threading_cutoff!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._SERIAL_CUTOFF"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._THREADING_OVERRIDE","page":"Fields","text":"_THREADING_OVERRIDE\n\nTask-local override for the threading decision: nothing follows Threads.nthreads(), true/false force threaded/serial. Set within a dynamic scope by with_threading.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._THREADING_OVERRIDE"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._assign!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_assign!(destination, source)\n\nFallback for a non-Broadcasted right-hand side (e.g. a bare component view from a transpose, or a constant): wrap it as an identity broadcast so the same threaded assignment path applies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._assign!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._assign!-Tuple{Any, Base.Broadcast.Broadcasted}","page":"Fields","text":"_assign!(destination, broadcasted)\n\nMaterialize the lazy broadcasted expression into destination, threaded over chunks on the CPU when _use_threads allows. The generic method is the standard fused Broadcast.materialize! — the safe polarity that keeps GPU arrays and unknown array wrappers on their normal broadcast path (no scalar indexing, honouring invariant 6). Only dense Arrays and Array-backed SubArrays take the threaded path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._assign!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._assign_threaded!-Tuple{Any, Base.Broadcast.Broadcasted}","page":"Fields","text":"_assign_threaded!(destination, broadcasted)\n\nThread a fused broadcast assignment over contiguous chunks of destination's linear index range. Each task converts its linear indices to Cartesian ones and writes the corresponding broadcast elements, so fusion is preserved (one Broadcasted tree, no temporaries) and the result is bitwise-identical to serial (each index is written by exactly one task). Cartesian indexing of the Broadcasted works on every supported Julia version and correctly remaps singleton operand axes — linear indexing of a multidimensional Broadcasted is only supported on Julia ≥ 1.12. Falls back to serial when the axes do not match (defensive; the field kernels always match).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._assign_threaded!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._axis_wavenumber_arrays-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray, Int64, T, Bool}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Fields","text":"_axis_wavenumber_arrays(reference, number_of_points, domain_length, half_dimension)\n\nBuild the per-axis derivative and squared wavenumber vectors with _axis_wavenumber_values on the host, then copy them onto the storage backend of reference (via similar + copyto!, avoiding scalar GPU writes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._axis_wavenumber_arrays"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._axis_wavenumber_values-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Int64, T, Bool}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Fields","text":"_axis_wavenumber_values(number_of_points, domain_length, half_dimension)\n\nReturn (derivative_wavenumber, squared_wavenumber) as host vectors for one spatial axis. half_dimension marks the real-FFT half axis, which stores only the nonnegative frequencies. On even-length axes the derivative wavenumber is zeroed at the Nyquist mode (so derivatives of real fields stay real) while the squared wavenumber keeps the true k² (correct Laplacian) — the deliberate Nyquist split, stored as two independent families.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._axis_wavenumber_values"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_broadcast_field_compatibility-Tuple{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldPhysical}","page":"Fields","text":"_check_broadcast_field_compatibility(destination, argument)\n\nCheck, during field broadcasting, that argument is compatible with destination: same-representation fields must pass check_field_compatibility; mixing physical and spectral representations throws; non-field arguments (real numbers, plain arrays) are accepted. Recurses into the operands of a Broadcasted argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_broadcast_field_compatibility"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_field_domain_compatibility","page":"Fields","text":"_check_field_domain_compatibility(a::AbstractFieldPhysical, b::AbstractFieldPhysical)\n_check_field_domain_compatibility(a::AbstractFieldSpectral, b::AbstractFieldSpectral)\n\nCheck that two fields in the same representation share scalar type, grid, and Fourier layout.\n\nTensor order is allowed to differ, so this is the check for representation-preserving operations that change order, such as gradient and divergence. Returns nothing or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_field_domain_compatibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_field_matches_context-Tuple{Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_check_field_matches_context(field::AbstractField, context)\n\nCheck that field was built for the same grid and Fourier layout as context.\n\nHere context is a precomputed spectral artifact carrying grid and fourier_layout fields, namely FieldTransformPlans or FieldSpectralMultipliers. Returns nothing or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_field_matches_context"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_multipliers_compatibility-Tuple{AbstractFieldSpectral, FieldSpectralMultipliers}","page":"Fields","text":"_check_multipliers_compatibility(field::AbstractFieldSpectral, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nCheck that a spectral-space field and multipliers share scalar type, grid, and Fourier layout. Returns nothing or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_multipliers_compatibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_same_domain-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_check_same_domain(a, b)\n\nThrow unless a and b share scalar type, grid, and Fourier layout (tensor order may differ). Shared body for the same-representation domain checks; the entry points that call it (_check_field_domain_compatibility, check_field_compatibility) are each restricted to a single representation, so a physical/spectral pair has no matching method and is rejected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_same_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._first_field-Tuple{Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}}","page":"Fields","text":"_first_field(x)\n\nReturn the first AbstractField found in x — a field, an arbitrary value, a Broadcasted tree, or an argument tuple — or nothing if there is none. Out-of-place field broadcasting uses it to pick the field whose grid/layout metadata seeds the allocated result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._first_field"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._index_ranges-Tuple{AbstractUnitRange, Integer}","page":"Fields","text":"_index_ranges(axis, n)\n\nPartition the index range axis into at most n contiguous, near-equal UnitRanges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._index_ranges"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._init_fft_threads-Tuple{}","page":"Fields","text":"_init_fft_threads()\n\nSet FFTW's thread count to match Julia's (one when threading is off). FFTW bakes the thread count into each plan, so this runs at package load, before any FieldTransformPlans is built. Retune afterwards with set_fft_threads!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._init_fft_threads"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._squared_wavenumber_broadcast-Tuple{FieldSpectralMultipliers}","page":"Fields","text":"_squared_wavenumber_broadcast(multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nReturn the total squared wavenumber k² = k₁² + k₂² + k₃² reshaped to broadcast over the three spatial dimensions. Used by the Laplacian, inverse Laplacian, and Helmholtz projection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._squared_wavenumber_broadcast"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._tensor2_component_data-Tuple{Union{TensorFieldPhysical{2, T} where T<:AbstractFloat, TensorFieldSpectral{2, T} where T<:AbstractFloat}, Any, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_tensor2_component_data(\n  field::Union{TensorFieldPhysical{2}, TensorFieldSpectral{2}},\n  first_index,\n  second_index,\n)\n\nReturn a view of the storage array corresponding to component (first_index, second_index) of a second-order tensor field.\n\nThe first three dimensions are spatial dimensions. The last two dimensions are the tensor-fiber indices. This helper is internal and works for second-order tensor fields in physical-space and spectral representations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._tensor2_component_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._to_broadcasted-Tuple{Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_to_broadcasted(expr)\n\nCodegen helper operating on expressions: rewrite a dotted expression — infix dotted operators (a .* b .+ c) and broadcast calls (f.(x)) — into the equivalent lazy Base.Broadcast.broadcasted(...) tree, recursing into operands and leaving non-broadcast subexpressions untouched. Lets generated and hand-written kernels route a fused assignment through _assign! without materializing intermediates (fusion is preserved).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._to_broadcasted"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._unwrap_broadcast_argument-Tuple{Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}}","page":"Fields","text":"_unwrap_broadcast_argument(x)\n\nReplace each field in a broadcast expression with its storage array — recursing through a Broadcasted tree and leaving non-field arguments untouched — so a field-level broadcast lowers to a plain array broadcast on the underlying backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._unwrap_broadcast_argument"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._use_threads-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Fields","text":"_use_threads(element_count)\n\nWhether an assignment over element_count elements should be threaded: at or above the serial cutoff, and either forced on or — under the automatic policy — Threads.nthreads() > 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._use_threads"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._vector_component_data-Tuple{Union{VectorFieldPhysical, VectorFieldSpectral}, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_vector_component_data(\n  field::Union{VectorFieldPhysical, VectorFieldSpectral},\n  component,\n)\n\nReturn a view of the storage array corresponding to vector component component.\n\nThe first three dimensions are spatial dimensions. The last dimension is the vector-fiber index. This helper is internal and works for vector fields in physical-space and spectral representations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._vector_component_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._wavenumber_broadcast_view-Tuple{FieldSpectralMultipliers, Val{1}}","page":"Fields","text":"_wavenumber_broadcast_view(multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers, ::Val{axis})\n\nReshape the per-axis derivative wavenumber array (axis = 1, 2, 3) so it broadcasts against the three spatial dimensions of a field in spectral representation. Used by the first-derivative spectral operators (gradient, divergence, curl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._wavenumber_broadcast_view"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.check_field_compatibility","page":"Fields","text":"check_field_compatibility(a::AbstractFieldPhysical, b::AbstractFieldPhysical)\ncheck_field_compatibility(a::AbstractFieldSpectral, b::AbstractFieldSpectral)\n\nCheck that two fields in the same representation share scalar type, tensor order, grid, and Fourier layout.\n\nThis is the check for operations that require fully matching operands. Returns nothing or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.check_field_compatibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.rfft_shape-Tuple{Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64}, Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64}}","page":"Fields","text":"rfft_shape(grid_size::NTuple{3, Int}, fft_dims::NTuple{3, Int})\n\nReturn the shape of the nonredundant real-to-complex Fourier representation of a three-dimensional real field.\n\nHere grid_size is the physical grid size and fft_dims is the order of transformed dimensions. The dimension fft_dims[1] is the real-FFT half dimension: it is stored with length grid_size[fft_dims[1]] ÷ 2 + 1, while the other dimensions keep their physical-grid lengths.\n\nReturns a three-tuple giving the spectral array size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.rfft_shape"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.similar_field","page":"Fields","text":"similar_field(reference::AbstractField, data)\n\nsimilar_field(reference::AbstractField, ::Val{R})\n\nConstruct a field with metadata copied from reference.\n\nWith storage array data, the field has the same representation and tensor order as reference. With Val{R}, new storage is allocated with the same representation and tensor order R.\n\nThis is used by out-of-place field broadcasting and by allocating algebraic operations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.similar_field"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.similar_physical_field","page":"Fields","text":"similar_physical_field(reference::AbstractFieldSpectral, data)\n\nConstruct a field represented in physical space with storage array data and metadata copied from the spectral-space reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.similar_physical_field"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.similar_spectral_field","page":"Fields","text":"similar_spectral_field(reference::AbstractFieldPhysical, data)\n\nConstruct a field represented in spectral space with storage array data and metadata copied from the physical-space reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.similar_spectral_field"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.validate_fft_dims-Tuple{Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64}}","page":"Fields","text":"validate_fft_dims(d::NTuple{3, Int})\n\nCheck whether d is a valid ordering of the three spatial Fourier-transform dimensions. Returns true or false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.validate_fft_dims"},{"category":"section","location":"#GBGPeriodicFields.jl","page":"Home","text":"GBGPeriodicFields.jl is a package for representing, transforming, and manipulating scalar, vector, and tensor fields on three-dimensional rectangular periodic domains.\n\nThe package is aimed primarily at fluid-dynamics and turbulence simulations. Fields are physical objects, while physical-space and nonredundant spectral representations are storage and manipulation choices. The implementation is designed for both CPU and GPU array backends, and the package is intended to support later Lagrangian-particle and interpolation tools.\n\nThis package is not intended to be a ready-to-use DNS solver; rather, it is a toolkit for building fluid-dynamics and general field solvers.","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"This is an unregistered package, so it is not listed in the General Julia registry. To install it, run:\n\n]add https://github.com/GrigorySarnitsky/GBGPeriodicFields.jl\n\nor\n\nusing Pkg\n\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/GrigorySarnitsky/GBGPeriodicFields.jl\")\n\nThen you can use it as usual:\n\nusing GBGPeriodicFields","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Main-components","page":"Home","text":"Fields: grids, Cartesian fiber types, field representations, FFT transforms, field utilities, and broadcasting support.\nFieldAlgebra: fiberwise algebraic operations on fields, including vector-space operations, scalar actions, vector products, tensor products, tensor contractions, norms, and tensor decompositions.\nDifferentialOperators: spectral differential operators — gradient, divergence, curl, Laplacian, inverse Laplacian, and Helmholtz projection.\n\nGlobal products, global norms, and integration-based adjoints are intentionally outside the algebra; spectral differential operators live in the separate DifferentialOperators module.","title":"Main components"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation-structure","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Documentation structure"},{"category":"section","location":"#General-documentation","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\"index.md\"]","title":"General documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"#API-reference","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\"api.md\", \"api/GBGPeriodicFields.md\", \"api/Fields.md\", \"api/FieldAlgebra.md\", \"api/DifferentialOperators.md\"]","title":"API reference"}]
}
